"comparison": "Both MU and AMD define their business structure by end-market segments (data center, client/PC, consumer), but they differ in organizational scope and specific product offerings within those markets. MU's Storage Business Unit (SBU) is a distinct operational unit focused solely on SSDs and NAND components across data center, PC, and consumer segments. In contrast, AMD organizes its entire company into four reportable segments (Data Center, Client, Gaming, Embedded), where 'Client' and 'Data Center' overlap with MU's markets but include CPUs and GPUs rather than storage. Specifically, MU identifies 'client SSDs' as part of the PC market (mu_2024_1_P041_S002), while AMD defines its Client segment as including 'CPUs, APUs, and chipsets for desktops and notebooks' (amd_2024_1_P013_S003). Similarly, while MU's data center market includes 'enterprise and cloud SSDs' (mu_2024_1_P041_S002), AMD's Data Center segment focuses on 'AI accelerators, server CPUs, GPUs... for data centers' (amd_2024_1_P013_S002).",
