"comparison": "Both MU and INTC discuss semiconductor manufacturing strategies and capacity, but from different business models. MU's SBU relies on internal or contracted NAND manufacturing to produce storage components. INTC explicitly describes its IDM 2.0 strategy to expand its 'foundry business for external customers' due to high capital requirements, offering 'wafer fabrication, packaging, chiplets, and software/services'. While MU's disclosure focuses on the *products* sold (SSDs/NAND) into markets, INTC's disclosure focuses on the *manufacturing service* model. INTC states: 'We are seeking to build a world-class foundry business also serving external customers... Our foundry offerings include four components: wafer fabrication, packaging, chiplets, and software and services' (intc_2024_1_P014_S003, intc_2024_1_P014_S004). MU's text describes the *result* of its manufacturing/supply chain: 'SBU includes SSDs and component-level storage solutions' (mu_2024_1_P041_S001). The core similarity is the semiconductor focus; the difference is MU's product-centric view vs. INTC's foundry-service-centric view.",
